From: turbinebmw <turbine.bmw@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 13:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ipa: simple: awb: Use grey zones and the illuminant locus

Grey world over the whole frame lets the dominant colour of a scene set the
white balance: on the Surface Pro 12 rear camera, a warm wooden floor and
cardboard turned white paper cyan.

Collect R, G and B sums over a 16x12 grid of zones in the software ISP
statistics, next to the global sums. With an illuminant locus in the
tuning file (the sensor's white point, R/G and B/G, per colour
temperature), the AWB averages only the zones that could be grey: lit,
unsaturated, and within `zoneDistance` of the locus, weighted by
brightness. It falls back to the whole frame when fewer than 8 qualify.
The estimate is then moved onto the locus, keeping up to `offLocus` of
its distance from it (LED and fluorescent light sits off the curve), the
colour temperature for the CCM comes from the locus instead of a generic
sRGB estimate, and `speed` smooths changes between frames. Without a
locus the algorithm is unchanged.

The IMX681 and OV13858 loci come from the chromaticities in the CCM
records of the modules' Windows tuning files. In blind comparisons the
new AWB fixed the rear camera's cyan whites and tied with grey world on
the front camera.
---
--- a/include/libcamera/internal/software_isp/swisp_stats.h
+++ b/include/libcamera/internal/software_isp/swisp_stats.h
@@ -43,6 +43,19 @@
 	 * \brief A histogram of luminance values of all the sampled pixels
 	 */
 	Histogram yHistogram;
+	/**
+	 * \brief Number of AWB zones across and down the stats window
+	 */
+	static constexpr unsigned int kAwbZonesX = 16;
+	static constexpr unsigned int kAwbZonesY = 12;
+	/**
+	 * \brief Sums of R, G and B (scaled like sum_) and the sample count of a zone
+	 */
+	using AwbZone = std::array<uint32_t, 4>;
+	/**
+	 * \brief Per-zone colour sums, row by row, for auto white balance
+	 */
+	std::array<AwbZone, kAwbZonesX * kAwbZonesY> awbZones;
 };
 
 } /* namespace libcamera */
